!id: MkSPgRXdzm
!front: State the law of sines for a nondegenerate triangle with sides \( a,b,c \) opposite angles \( A,B,C \).
!back:
\[ \frac{a}{\sin A}=\frac{b}{\sin B}=\frac{c}{\sin C}. \]

![A nondegenerate triangle ABC with side a opposite angle A, side b opposite angle B, and side c opposite angle C; each vertex angle and its opposite side have matching labels](img/law-of-sines.svg)
~~~

!id: 2lNLGXvKnj
!front: State the law of cosines for side \( c \), opposite angle \( C \). How does it include the Pythagorean theorem?
!back:
\[ c^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\cos C. \]

![A generic triangle ABC with sides a and b meeting at angle C and opposite side c](img/law-of-cosines.svg)

When \( C=\pi/2 \), the cosine term vanishes, giving \( c^2=a^2+b^2 \).

![The same triangle with C a right angle, so c is the hypotenuse](img/law-of-cosines-right.svg)
~~~

!id: QHWK5EoZgj
!front: What is the area of a triangle with sides \( a,b \) enclosing angle \( C \)?
!back:
\[ \frac12 ab\sin C \]
Taking \( a \) as the base, the corresponding height is \( b\sin C \), so the area is \( \frac12 a(b\sin C) \).

![Triangle ABC with base BC of length a, side CA of length b, and included angle C; a perpendicular from A to the line through BC marks the height h equals b times sine C, with the altitude foot shown on the segment BC for an acute example](img/triangle-area-sine.svg)
~~~
